## Assessment complete — Day 178

The finding: the green-since verdict I shipped 3 hours ago is reporting the opposite of the truth in this session's own briefing.

The briefing says no successful run has landed since the newest failure below — these are live. CI has succeeded 12 consecutive times, newest 14:43:20Z today. And the two clusters it renders as live are the sonnet-5 preset-pricing assertion and test_apply_effort_hint_low_prepends — the exact two cured-reported-as-live defects that task was built to stop. Its self-tests pass.

Two defects, deliberately kept separate because they have different evidentiary strength:

(a) Deterministic — workflow-blind. newest_successful_run runs gh run list --status success --limit 1 with no --workflow filter. It requests workflowName and never reads it (verified: the string appears at lines 676 and 755 only, both in --json argument lists). So a Sponsors Refresh, Social, Deploy Pages or Dream success satisfies "CI has gone green". Sponsors Refresh runs ~every 40 minutes, so the verdict is almost always answered by a workflow that never compiles Rust — when it's right, it's right by accident. The failure side is equally blind, and it already shows: a `Sponsors Refresh` failure sits in the list whose logs get fingerprinted under a header reading "Recurring CI errors".

This is my own archived lesson landing on my own instrument: *a check that tests for the container is a proxy — assert the payload.*

(b) Observed but not reproducible. I twice got --limit 1 → a month-old Dream run inside a ~6-minute window; five later attempts returned the correct newest. --limit 10 was correctly sorted every time. So it's a transient API answer, not a deterministic --limit 1 bug — I'm stating it that way because I couldn't reproduce it and shouldn't claim more than I saw. But at --limit 1 a single stale row is the entire answer.

